WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.550 --> 00:00:03.462
This is where the well meets the template.

2
00:00:03.502 --> 00:00:10.143
The conductor housing lands in a slot of
the template structure, and lower down,

3
00:00:10.202 --> 00:00:15.153
the conductor is guided by a ring
in the bottom of the structure.

4
00:00:15.193 --> 00:00:20.483
When the riser pulls on the wellhead,
the conductor pushes on that ring.

5
00:00:21.717 --> 00:00:25.854
So when has this interface
failed, according to the standard?

6
00:00:25.894 --> 00:00:31.042
When the steel reaches 0.2 %
plastic strain, the yield strength?

7
00:00:31.082 --> 00:00:38.192
Or at a strain limit, like the 5 %, or the
2 %, that people attribute to Eurocode 3,

8
00:00:38.232 --> 00:00:39.654
part 1-5?

9
00:00:40.617 --> 00:00:44.067
In this follow-up to the
acceptance criteria video,

10
00:00:44.107 --> 00:00:47.107
we find out which standard
owns the interface,

11
00:00:47.227 --> 00:00:52.337
what failure means on each side of
it, what Eurocode 3 actually says,

12
00:00:52.377 --> 00:00:54.908
and we check the slot ring both ways.

13
00:00:59.050 --> 00:01:04.019
NORSOK U-001 names the
contacts in Figure J.1.

14
00:01:04.059 --> 00:01:09.009
Contact C is between the template
structure and the conductor housing,

15
00:01:09.159 --> 00:01:12.389
and contact D is at the
bottom of the structure.

16
00:01:12.429 --> 00:01:18.229
When the template is in the load path,
load path three runs from the wellhead,

17
00:01:18.279 --> 00:01:23.557
through the conductor, into the guide
structure and down to the seabed.

18
00:01:23.837 --> 00:01:27.367
How much load goes that
way is the load share.

19
00:01:27.407 --> 00:01:31.847
It depends on the tolerances and
on the relative stiffness of well,

20
00:01:31.887 --> 00:01:33.477
template and soil.

21
00:01:34.417 --> 00:01:39.865
The interface has two sides, and
each side has its own rule book.

22
00:01:39.905 --> 00:01:43.995
The wellhead side, the conductor
housing and the conductor,

23
00:01:44.045 --> 00:01:50.629
is checked to Annex B of U-001 and
the pipe formulas of clause B.9.

24
00:01:50.669 --> 00:01:56.379
That was the first video: the yield
strength at 0.2 % plastic strain,

25
00:01:56.419 --> 00:01:58.786
and capacities found as loads.

26
00:01:58.826 --> 00:02:01.352
The template side is different.

27
00:02:01.632 --> 00:02:13.022
Clause 13.3.6.1 says that structural design
shall follow ISO 13628-15, clause 5.4,

28
00:02:13.062 --> 00:02:15.617
and NORSOK N-001.

29
00:02:15.657 --> 00:02:24.636
N-001 sends steel design on to N-004,
and N-004 builds on Eurocode 3.

30
00:02:24.676 --> 00:02:31.178
So U-001 has no failure criterion
of its own for the template steel.

31
00:02:31.458 --> 00:02:33.343
It hands it over.

32
00:02:34.283 --> 00:02:38.969
Before any stress, the interface
can fail as a mechanism.

33
00:02:39.009 --> 00:02:42.949
The well grows with temperature,
and the template settles.

34
00:02:42.989 --> 00:02:49.993
Clause 13.3.6.2 asks the template design
to account for a relative vertical

35
00:02:50.033 --> 00:02:55.469
displacement of 100 millimetres
between template and conductor,

36
00:02:55.599 --> 00:02:57.603
unless otherwise specified.

37
00:02:57.643 --> 00:03:04.433
Note d of Table A.2 makes this a
serviceability check, with load factor 1.0:

38
00:03:04.643 --> 00:03:08.322
the margins to the upper and
lower end stops are checked.

39
00:03:08.362 --> 00:03:12.152
If an end stop is reached,
the template is preloaded,

40
00:03:12.192 --> 00:03:16.545
and that force goes into separate
ultimate limit state load cases.

41
00:03:16.585 --> 00:03:21.575
And the installed template may
not lean more than 0.3 degrees,

42
00:03:21.635 --> 00:03:24.084
unless the tolerance loops allow it.

43
00:03:28.550 --> 00:03:32.611
On the template side there
are no design factors.

44
00:03:32.651 --> 00:03:35.646
It is load and resistance factor design.

45
00:03:35.686 --> 00:03:42.536
The loads are multiplied by the load
factors of N-001, which Table A.2 lists:

46
00:03:42.736 --> 00:03:47.416
1.3 on the extreme riser loads
in the ultimate limit state,

47
00:03:47.476 --> 00:03:50.508
1.0 in the accidental case.

48
00:03:50.548 --> 00:03:53.828
The resistance is divided
by a material factor:

49
00:03:54.058 --> 00:03:59.861
1.15 for steel in the ultimate
limit state, in N-004,

50
00:03:59.901 --> 00:04:03.954
and 1.0 in the accidental limit state.

51
00:04:04.917 --> 00:04:06.783
And the yield strength?

52
00:04:06.823 --> 00:04:16.467
For a 40 millimetre plate of S355, the
nominal yield strength, fy, is 345 MPa.

53
00:04:16.507 --> 00:04:21.317
Eurocode 3 takes it from the product
standard: the upper yield strength,

54
00:04:21.357 --> 00:04:27.313
or the 0.2 % proof strength when the
steel shows no distinct yield point.

55
00:04:27.353 --> 00:04:33.358
So yes, it is the same
idea as the 0.2 % offset.

56
00:04:33.398 --> 00:04:38.725
And it is reached at a
strain of only 0.16 %.

57
00:04:39.683 --> 00:04:42.433
The first Eurocode check is elastic.

58
00:04:42.473 --> 00:04:47.633
Clause 6.2.1, paragraph five, of part 1-1:

59
00:04:47.873 --> 00:04:54.762
the von Mises stress at the critical
point shall not exceed fy divided by γM0.

60
00:04:54.802 --> 00:04:58.545
Here is the lower slot
ring, cut from that plate,

61
00:04:58.585 --> 00:05:02.995
with the conductor pushing
on it between two struts.

62
00:05:03.275 --> 00:05:07.445
At the design contact force of 1.6 MN,

63
00:05:07.535 --> 00:05:13.035
the outer edge behind the
contact reaches 416 MPa.

64
00:05:13.075 --> 00:05:15.286
The limit is 300.

65
00:05:15.326 --> 00:05:18.065
Utilisation, 1.39.

66
00:05:18.105 --> 00:05:21.848
So by the elastic check,
the ring has failed.

67
00:05:22.128 --> 00:05:25.783
But that check stops at the
first point that yields.

68
00:05:25.823 --> 00:05:30.415
It is the most conservative
option, not the only one.

69
00:05:34.550 --> 00:05:40.630
For a plated detail in a finite element
model, Eurocode 3 offers another route:

70
00:05:40.910 --> 00:05:45.714
Annex C of part 1-5, finite
element methods of analysis.

71
00:05:45.754 --> 00:05:49.162
It is an informative annex,
so the National Annex

72
00:05:49.202 --> 00:05:52.785
and your design basis
decide whether it is used.

73
00:05:52.825 --> 00:05:56.445
For a stocky part that cannot
buckle, like this ring,

74
00:05:56.485 --> 00:05:59.644
a materially non-linear
analysis is enough.

75
00:05:59.684 --> 00:06:03.099
Where buckling is possible,
the geometric non-linearity

76
00:06:03.139 --> 00:06:06.667
and the imperfections
must be included as well.

77
00:06:07.617 --> 00:06:13.577
Clause C.6 gives the material models:
elastic plastic without strain hardening,

78
00:06:13.617 --> 00:06:17.457
with a nominal plateau slope,
with linear strain hardening,

79
00:06:17.497 --> 00:06:20.402
or a true stress strain curve from tests.

80
00:06:20.442 --> 00:06:26.842
They use characteristic values: fy itself,
not divided by the material factor.

81
00:06:26.882 --> 00:06:31.182
Clause C.7 puts the factored
design loads into the model,

82
00:06:31.322 --> 00:06:34.420
multiplied by one load multiplier, α,

83
00:06:34.460 --> 00:06:39.062
which the analysis increases
until a limit state is reached.

84
00:06:40.317 --> 00:06:46.326
And here is the clause you heard
about: C.8, the limit state criteria.

85
00:06:46.366 --> 00:06:48.976
Where the structure is
susceptible to buckling,

86
00:06:49.036 --> 00:06:52.052
the criterion is reaching
the maximum load.

87
00:06:52.092 --> 00:06:55.204
In regions in tension,
it is reaching a limiting

88
00:06:55.244 --> 00:06:58.134
value of the principal membrane strain.

89
00:06:58.174 --> 00:07:04.318
Note one: the National Annex may specify
that value, and 5 % is recommended.

90
00:07:04.358 --> 00:07:09.988
Note two: other criteria may be used,
for example reaching the yield criterion,

91
00:07:10.028 --> 00:07:12.101
or limiting the yielded zone.

92
00:07:12.141 --> 00:07:16.021
Membrane means the strain at
mid thickness of the shell,

93
00:07:16.201 --> 00:07:20.227
not the surface value with
plate bending added on top.

94
00:07:21.483 --> 00:07:22.947
Let's run it.

95
00:07:22.987 --> 00:07:28.259
Up to 83 % of the design
load, the ring is elastic.

96
00:07:28.299 --> 00:07:31.399
Then the outer edge
behind the contact yields,

97
00:07:31.519 --> 00:07:38.566
and at 1.25 times the design
load it has 0.2 % plastic strain.

98
00:07:38.606 --> 00:07:43.075
The plastic zones spread, behind
the contact and at the struts.

99
00:07:43.115 --> 00:07:49.449
Without strain hardening, the curve
flattens at 1.54: a plastic mechanism.

100
00:07:49.489 --> 00:07:52.122
With linear hardening, it keeps rising.

101
00:07:52.162 --> 00:07:57.102
There is no peak and no buckling,
so the 5 % strain decides:

102
00:07:57.292 --> 00:08:00.821
at 1.76 times the design load.

103
00:08:01.783 --> 00:08:04.573
Clause C.9 sets the margin.

104
00:08:04.613 --> 00:08:10.531
It should be verified that the
multiplier at the limit state, αu,

105
00:08:10.571 --> 00:08:13.695
is greater than α1 times α2.

106
00:08:13.735 --> 00:08:19.618
α1 covers the uncertainty of the
FE model, calibrated against tests.

107
00:08:19.658 --> 00:08:26.428
α2 covers the scatter of loads and
resistance: γM1 where instability governs,

108
00:08:26.468 --> 00:08:29.145
γM2 where fracture governs.

109
00:08:29.185 --> 00:08:36.707
With α1 equal to one, for a benchmarked
model, and α2 no more than 1.3,

110
00:08:36.747 --> 00:08:43.416
αu of 1.76 passes, and so
does 1.53 without hardening.

111
00:08:43.456 --> 00:08:49.046
So the ring that failed the elastic
check is accepted by the non-linear one,

112
00:08:49.106 --> 00:08:50.440
with margin.

113
00:08:54.883 --> 00:08:56.784
Now the 2 %.

114
00:08:56.824 --> 00:09:01.397
Annex C contains one strain
value, the recommended 5 %.

115
00:09:01.437 --> 00:09:05.558
We found no 2 % criterion in part 1-5.

116
00:09:05.598 --> 00:09:11.987
If someone quotes one, ask which document
it comes from, and which strain it limits.

117
00:09:12.027 --> 00:09:14.196
And what would it change here?

118
00:09:14.476 --> 00:09:20.755
With hardening, stopping at 2 % instead
of five lowers the capacity by 10 %.

119
00:09:20.795 --> 00:09:25.799
Without hardening, by only 2 %,
because the curve is already flat.

120
00:09:26.750 --> 00:09:29.757
There is a catch with every strain limit.

121
00:09:29.797 --> 00:09:34.947
Without hardening, the same
load, 1.5 times the design load,

122
00:09:35.017 --> 00:09:41.570
gives 1.4 % strain with coarse
elements, and 2.4 % with fine ones.

123
00:09:41.610 --> 00:09:45.773
The load at the limit hardly
moves, but the strain does.

124
00:09:45.813 --> 00:09:48.824
With hardening, the
mesh no longer matters.

125
00:09:48.864 --> 00:09:54.074
So a strain limit always comes
with a material model, a mesh,

126
00:09:54.124 --> 00:09:56.608
and a definition of the strain.

127
00:09:56.888 --> 00:10:02.405
The second generation of Eurocode 3
develops this into a part of its own,

128
00:10:02.445 --> 00:10:07.867
EN 1993-1-14, published in 2025.

129
00:10:07.907 --> 00:10:11.390
Check which edition your
design basis calls for.

130
00:10:15.550 --> 00:10:18.648
So, when has the template
interface failed?

131
00:10:18.688 --> 00:10:24.674
As a mechanism, when the relative displacement
uses up the margin to the end stops.

132
00:10:24.714 --> 00:10:30.724
On the wellhead side, when the load
exceeds Fd times the capacity of Annex B,

133
00:10:30.894 --> 00:10:35.387
with the yield strength
at 0.2 % plastic strain.

134
00:10:35.427 --> 00:10:39.055
On the template side,
by the elastic route,

135
00:10:39.095 --> 00:10:43.915
when the von Mises stress at
a point reaches fy over γM.

136
00:10:43.955 --> 00:10:45.695
And by the non-linear route,

137
00:10:45.735 --> 00:10:50.265
at the maximum load or at 5
% principal membrane strain,

138
00:10:50.395 --> 00:10:54.065
with αu greater than α1 times α2.

139
00:10:54.105 --> 00:10:58.973
Two percent is not a criterion
of Eurocode 3, part 1-5.

140
00:10:59.917 --> 00:11:05.334
The ring, its load and the factors
in this video are teaching inputs.

141
00:11:05.374 --> 00:11:08.546
The clauses are on
screen, and the notes list

142
00:11:08.586 --> 00:11:11.566
what was checked and against which source.

143
00:11:11.606 --> 00:11:16.540
Use the editions and the National
Annex of your own design basis.

